{
  "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
  "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner Charles Borden, Jr.",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0000-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Borden argued that this offense is not a violent felony under ACCA's elements clause because a mental state of recklessness suffces for conviction."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States (Government)",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0000-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 2,
        "start": 2
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The District Court disagreed and sentenced Borden as a career offender."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0000-0004",
      "kind": "fact",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 6,
        "start": 6
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Petitioner Charles Borden, Jr., pleaded guilty to a felon-in-possession charge, and the Government sought an enhanced sentence under ACCA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0001-0001",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "According to Borden, that word means \u201cin opposition to,\u201d and so \u201cintroduces the target of the preceding action.\u201d Brief for Petitioner 19 (citing dictionaries)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0001-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 12,
        "start": 12
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Government responds that \u201cagainst\u201d instead means \u201cmak[ing] contact with,\u201d and so introduces the mere recipient of force rather than its \u201cintended target.\u201d Brief for United States 23\u201324 (also citing dictionaries)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "United States",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.9,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "To all of this, the Government offers one main response: this Court's decision in Voisine."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0002-0006",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 28,
        "start": 28
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The question presented here is whether the elements clause encompasses petitioner's conviction under Tennessee law for reckless aggravated assault. It does not."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0002-0007",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 29,
        "start": 29
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The problem is that Johnson held that the residual clause is \u201cunconstitutionally vague\u201d and thus unenforceable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Thomas",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0002-0008",
      "kind": "concurrence",
      "opinion_part": "concurrence",
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Second, even assuming that the petitioner's plea for facial relief was cognizable, Johnson deviated from the usual legal standard."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0003-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 31,
        "start": 31
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "I hesitate to give petitioner the beneft of Johnson, because his crime is a \u201cviolent felony\u201d as Congress defned the term."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0003-0002",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 35,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The Court's decision disregards bedrock principles and longstanding terminology of criminal law, misconstrues ACCA's text, and waves away the Court's own recent precedent."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0004-0001",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 42,
        "start": 42
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Finally, state practice confrms that the phrase \u201cagainst the person of another\u201d in ACCA refects a longstanding term of art and is not an oblique and novel way of excluding reckless offenses."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0004-0002",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 43,
        "start": 43
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Congress used the phrase \u201cagainst the person of another\u201d in the use-of-force clause to describe the \u201ccrime[s]\u201d that are covered by ACCA."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0004-0006",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 47,
        "start": 47
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "In short, in enacting ACCA, \u201cCongress must have known it was sweeping in some persons who had engaged in reckless conduct.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0004-0008",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 49,
        "start": 49
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Most tellingly, if one wants to fnd a really good example of the ordinary meaning, look no further than the Court's opinion in Voisine fve years ago. There, the Court stated twice in its opinion\u2014in the frst and last paragraphs\u2014that the use-of-force statute at issue there covered offenses where an individual recklessly used physical force \u201cagainst a domestic relation.\u201d"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0005-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 50,
        "start": 50
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "One can recklessly use force against the person of another (or against the property of another)."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0006-0000",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 60,
        "start": 60
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "To begin with, when a statute explicitly defnes a term,\nwe generally follow that statutory defnition. See Digital\nRealty Trust, Inc. v. Somers, 583 U. S. 149, 160 (2018). In\nACCA, Congress defned the term \u201cviolent felony\u201d to include\noffenses that involve the \u201cuse of physical force against the\nperson of another.\u201d And as explained above, that defnition\nencompasses reckless offenses like reckless assault and reck-\nless homicide."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0006-0003",
      "kind": "dissent",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 64,
        "start": 64
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Under the Court's decision today, however, not one of those\ndefendants committed a \u201cviolent felony\u201d for purposes of\nACCA because they supposedly did not commit an offense\nthat necessarily entailed the use of force against the person\nof another ."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kavanaugh",
      "case_id": "2020-19-5410",
      "confidence": 0.99,
      "document_hash": "e5b53a3d09a8a7fb848072e1a55f142f73034f96e3105e553d98baa3455d5e3f",
      "evidence_id": "ev-e5b53a3d09a8-0006-0001",
      "kind": "stated_consequence",
      "opinion_part": "dissent",
      "pages": {
        "end": 67,
        "start": 67
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "According to that Sentencing Commission report,\n59% of ACCA defendants released between 2009 and 2011\nwere re-arrested within eight years of their release from fed-\neral prison, most commonly for assault and most commonly\nwithin 18 months of release."
    }
  ],
  "schema_version": "1.0.0"
}
